---
// FAQ. Four questions; three of them hold the questions that belong with them (QA.astro has the two
// levels). The first answer is the only place where Eduardo speaks in the first person.
import QA, { type Item } from '../QA.astro';
import { SKILL_CONTRIBUTING, SKILL_ISSUES, SKILL_LICENSE, SKILL_REPO } from '../../data/site';

const items: Item[] = [
  {
    id: 'faq-why',
    q: 'How did this start?',
    a: [
      'It started as a tool to help me study. With the textbooks I was learning plenty of words, but when I tried to speak I couldn’t connect them, and my progress felt stuck. So I built this skill to learn by situation: everything around one moment, so I could actually talk about it.',
      'I wrote my own course with it, 67 lessons covering HSK 1 and 2. I remembered words better and connected ideas faster. That’s my experience, not a promise.',
      'A friend who was also studying tried it, liked it, and switched to learning with the skill. That’s when I opened it up to anyone.',
      `The goal is a clear, direct way to learn Mandarin, closer to how I learned English, free for anyone who uses Claude. Anyone who studies with it can shape it for the next person. Suggestions, new situations and fixes are all welcome <a href="${SKILL_ISSUES}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">on GitHub ↗</a>.`,
    ],
    subs: [
      {
        id: 'faq-built',
        q: 'How is it being built?',
        a: [
          'One version at a time. The words of each level were grouped by hand into situations and checked by script so none is missing. Each new version is tested on simulated lessons before it goes out, to catch lessons that use words you haven’t met or forget the older ones.',
          `It’s far from finished: what really shapes it is people studying with it and saying what felt off. Everything, tests included, is <a href="${SKILL_REPO}#how-its-made" target="_blank" rel="noopener">on GitHub ↗</a>.`,
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'faq-levels',
    q: 'Which levels does it cover?',
    a: [
      'HSK 1 to 4 are ready: 2,000 words, grouped by hand into 134 situations. Each level opens with a short jump start: the words every later lesson uses.',
      'When you finish a level, the course moves on to the next one, and words from the level you finished keep coming back. For HSK 5 and 6 the official word lists are included, and the skill builds the map when you start the level.',
      'The word list is the HSK syllabus released in November 2025.',
    ],
    subs: [
      {
        id: 'faq-start',
        q: 'Can I start in the middle?',
        a: [
          'Yes. Tell the skill the level or the lesson you want to pick up from, and the course starts there. Words from the levels below count as known, so the lessons use them from day one.',
          'You can also move a situation earlier or later, or change how many words you take per lesson, at any point.',
        ],
      },
      {
        id: 'faq-language',
        q: 'What language are the lessons in?',
        a: [
          'Yours. Explanations, meanings, questions and corrections come in the language you write in, and you translate from it into Mandarin. This site is in English, but your lessons don’t have to be.',
          'You answer in Mandarin from the first lesson. You can type pinyin at the start, then switch to the Chinese keyboard when you’re ready.',
        ],
      },
      {
        id: 'faq-length',
        q: 'How long is a lesson?',
        a: [
          'About an hour: a review, three rounds of practice and some listening. That’s what it takes to make real progress with a few new words. The first lessons are shorter, and you can always ask for a shorter session.',
          'Inside the skill, a situation is called a lesson and each day’s session a part.',
        ],
      },
      {
        id: 'faq-saved',
        q: 'Where does my course live?',
        a: [
          'In your Claude project. The skill keeps one small file there with your settings, your lesson list and what you’ve finished. Open a new chat in the same project, on any device, and say “next lesson”. There’s nothing to attach or paste.',
          'The skill also runs in Claude Code and Codex, where the course is saved in your working folder. I test it mostly in the Claude app.',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'faq-not',
    q: 'What doesn’t it do?',
    a: [
      'It doesn’t check your pronunciation or tones. Speaking practice is up to you.',
      'It doesn’t replace flashcards for long-term memory. Older words come back inside lessons, but the skill doesn’t schedule every word for you. Ask for flashcards and it gives you a lesson’s words, ready to import into Anki.',
      'It isn’t finished. The people who study with it are the ones testing it, and it keeps changing with what they find. The research behind its ideas is <a href="#sources-method">listed below</a>.',
      'It doesn’t promise fluency or a passing score. It covers the vocabulary. The exam also tests grammar, handwriting and speaking.',
    ],
  },
  {
    id: 'faq-free',
    q: 'Is it really free?',
    a: [
      'Yes. Free forever, and yours to keep. Study with it, change your own copy, read how it’s made. You need a Claude account, and a voice provider, if you connect one, has its own plans.',
    ],
    subs: [
      {
        id: 'faq-help',
        q: 'Can I help?',
        a: [
          `Please do. Say what felt off: a word in the wrong situation, a lesson that dragged, a situation that’s missing. It all starts with <a href="${SKILL_ISSUES}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">an issue on GitHub ↗</a>, in any language.`,
          `What you send becomes part of the project, and you’re credited for it. <a href="${SKILL_CONTRIBUTING}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">How contributing works ↗</a>`,
        ],
      },
      {
        id: 'faq-reuse',
        q: 'Can I reuse the maps or the lessons?',
        a: [
          'That’s the one thing we ask you not to do. The maps and the lesson texts belong to the project, so they can’t be copied into another product or fed to an AI to mass-produce copies. It’s what keeps the work of everyone who helps from being turned into AI slop.',
          `The method itself is yours to borrow: if you want to build your own course by situation, start from your own words. The details are in <a href="${SKILL_LICENSE}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">the license ↗</a>.`,
        ],
      },
      {
        id: 'faq-official',
        q: 'Is it official?',
        a: [
          'No. mandarinmaxxing is an independent project by Gautech. It uses the official HSK word list and isn’t affiliated with the organization that runs the test.',
        ],
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<QA id="faq" kicker="FAQ" title="Questions" items={items} section />
